# COURSE MATES ULTIMATE UPGRADE: COMPLETE PRIORITIZED ENGINEERING TASK LIST

**Repository:** https://github.com/cromUs2511/CourseMates/

## ROLE

Act as a principal software architect, senior full-stack engineer, backend engineer, game systems engineer, cybersecurity specialist, QA engineer, DevOps engineer, performance engineer, and product designer.

Your mission is to transform the existing CourseMates repository into its strongest possible version: a polished, reliable, secure, scalable, maintainable, and feature-rich anonymous student communication and social gaming platform.

Your objective is not merely to add more features. Your objective is to make every existing feature work correctly, eliminate architectural weaknesses, improve user experience, strengthen security, and prepare the platform for real-world adoption.

## NON-NEGOTIABLE RULES

1. Inspect the actual current repository before making assumptions.
2. Do not assume that README documentation accurately reflects the current implementation.
3. Preserve all working features and the established CourseMates visual identity.
4. Do not rewrite the entire application unless a documented technical reason justifies it.
5. Do not remove existing functionality to make tests pass.
6. Do not add unnecessary frameworks, services, dependencies, or programming languages.
7. Do not claim a feature works unless its implementation and behavior have been verified.
8. Do not claim performance, scalability, or security improvements without supporting evidence.
9. Preserve the intended anonymous and privacy-conscious nature of the application.
10. Make changes incrementally, run tests, fix regressions, and document results.
11. Prioritize critical defects before introducing new features.
12. Never expose secrets, credentials, private messages, or sensitive personal information.
13. Do not deploy breaking changes to production without appropriate verification and authorization.
14. Do not stop after producing an audit report. After the audit, proceed with the highest-priority safe implementation tasks.
15. If blocked by missing credentials, unavailable infrastructure, or an external dependency, document the blocker and continue with independent tasks.

# PHASE 1: COMPLETE REPOSITORY AUDIT — P0

1. Inspect the complete repository structure.
2. Identify the frontend, backend, API routes, WebSocket handlers, databases, caches, and external services.
3. Map the application startup sequence and major request flows.
4. Inspect all environment variables and configuration files without exposing secret values.
5. Review package scripts, build commands, dependencies, and runtime versions.
6. Inspect matchmaking, session management, room lifecycle, messaging, and reconnection.
7. Inspect every advertised multiplayer game and its server-side implementation.
8. Inspect media uploads, music synchronization, AI integrations, and moderation.
9. Inspect administrator functionality and access controls.
10. Review automated tests, CI workflows, deployment configuration, and known defects.
11. Identify incomplete features, duplicated logic, dead code, vulnerable dependencies, and architectural bottlenecks.
12. Produce a prioritized defect register containing severity, affected files, evidence, reproduction steps, root cause, proposed fix, and acceptance criteria.
13. Distinguish confirmed defects from suspected problems.
14. Identify existing improvements that are already complete so they are not unnecessarily reimplemented.

# PHASE 2: FIX CORE MESSAGING — P0

1. Ensure messages have stable unique identifiers.
2. Prevent duplicate messages caused by retries or reconnects.
3. Preserve message ordering within each conversation.
4. Define reliable sent, delivered, and seen states.
5. Ensure read receipts and unread counts remain consistent.
6. Fix typing indicators and their expiration.
7. Fix scrolling, new-message indicators, and jump-to-latest behavior.
8. Implement consistent Today and Yesterday separators.
9. Handle network interruptions and temporary disconnections.
10. Recover conversation state correctly after reconnection.
11. Prevent stale WebSocket events from modifying the wrong conversation.
12. Validate message permissions and payloads on the server.
13. Enforce message-size limits and safe input handling.
14. Handle simultaneous joins, leaves, and message submissions.
15. Ensure failed requests provide useful errors and safe retry behavior.
16. Test messaging across separate browsers, devices, and mobile layouts.

# PHASE 3: FIX SESSIONS AND MATCHMAKING — P0

1. Establish a consistent session lifecycle.
2. Prevent ghost users and stale matchmaking entries.
3. Handle browser refreshes and reconnections reliably.
4. Prevent duplicate or conflicting matchmaking requests.
5. Handle simultaneous matchmaking operations safely.
6. Implement reliable queue cancellation and timeout handling.
7. Prevent incompatible multiple-room membership.
8. Define skip, leave, block, and rematch behavior.
9. Prevent users from re-entering blocked interactions through trivial session changes.
10. Implement room expiration and abandoned-session cleanup.
11. Define reconnect grace periods and disconnected-player handling.
12. Ensure room state cannot be accessed by unauthorized users.
13. Handle server restarts without leaving invalid session references.
14. Test matchmaking races, repeated requests, and simultaneous disconnects.

# PHASE 4: REPAIR AND PERFECT ALL EIGHT GAMES — P0

First verify which games are currently implemented and identify their actual defects.

## UNO
1. Validate legal card plays and turn progression.
2. Verify draw behavior, penalties, and supported stacking rules.
3. Fix final-card and winner detection.
4. Handle disconnected players and invalid actions.
5. Verify rematches and complete match recovery.

## Tic-Tac-Toe
1. Prevent occupied-cell moves.
2. Enforce server-authoritative turn order.
3. Correct win and draw detection.
4. Synchronize both players' boards and results.
5. Verify invitations, rematches, and reconnection.

## Rock Paper Scissors
1. Fix best-of-three round progression.
2. Handle ties and repeated rounds.
3. Synchronize scores and final results.
4. Prevent duplicate submissions.
5. Verify rematches and disconnect handling.

## Connect Four
1. Prevent interaction during the opponent's turn.
2. Validate all moves on the server.
3. Handle full columns and invalid moves.
4. Correct horizontal, vertical, and diagonal win detection.
5. Handle draws, reconnection, and rematches.

## Chess
1. Correct piece rendering and board synchronization.
2. Implement or verify complete legal-move validation.
3. Handle check, checkmate, and stalemate.
4. Support castling, en passant, and promotion.
5. Handle draws and other applicable chess rules.
6. Prevent illegal moves and unauthorized board modifications.
7. Test complete matches, reconnection, and rematches.

## Trivia
1. Fix question progression.
2. Synchronize questions, answers, timers, and scores.
3. Validate answers on the server.
4. Handle timeouts and disconnected players.
5. Prevent duplicate scoring.
6. Ensure the game completes correctly when questions are exhausted.

## Would You Rather
1. Synchronize prompts and round transitions.
2. Validate player submissions and voting.
3. Display results consistently.
4. Prevent duplicate or late votes.
5. Handle disconnections and rematches.

## Draw & Guess
1. Synchronize drawing events and canvas state.
2. Enforce drawing permissions.
3. Synchronize guesses, rounds, timers, and scores.
4. Prevent unauthorized drawing-event injection.
5. Handle disconnections and round transitions.
6. Verify complete matches and rematches.

## Shared game requirements
1. Create reusable game-room and lifecycle logic.
2. Keep authoritative game state on the server.
3. Validate all moves and transitions on the server.
4. Prevent duplicate actions and stale-state updates.
5. Support invitations, acceptance, cancellation, and rematches.
6. Handle abandoned matches, forfeits, timeouts, and draws.
7. Restore valid game state after reconnection.
8. Standardize game events, error responses, and results.
9. Add complete automated tests for every advertised game.
10. Verify each game on desktop and mobile using separate player sessions.

# PHASE 5: SECURITY AND ABUSE PREVENTION — P0

1. Perform a security threat assessment of the entire application.
2. Enforce server-side authorization for every protected operation.
3. Secure session credentials, cookies, and token handling.
4. Validate WebSocket origins and connection permissions.
5. Validate all API inputs, WebSocket events, and uploaded files.
6. Implement rate limiting for sensitive operations.
7. Prevent spam, flooding, matchmaking abuse, and resource exhaustion.
8. Prevent game cheating and unauthorized state changes.
9. Harden administrator and moderator permissions.
10. Secure reporting, blocking, bans, and moderation workflows.
11. Protect against injection, cross-site scripting, CSRF where applicable, and unsafe redirects.
12. Configure appropriate security headers and transport security.
13. Keep secrets out of frontend bundles, source code, logs, and version control.
14. Scan the repository and Git history for exposed credentials.
15. Rotate any credentials found to have been exposed.
16. Audit dependencies for known vulnerabilities.
17. Secure file uploads and prevent unauthorized media access.
18. Restrict sensitive information in application logs.
19. Test authorization boundaries and common attack scenarios.
20. Arrange independent security testing before broad public release.

# PHASE 6: PRIVACY AND MODERATION — P0

1. Document exactly what user data the application collects.
2. Minimize the collection of personally identifying information.
3. Define retention and deletion rules for messages, media, sessions, and logs.
4. Preserve the intended ephemeral nature of anonymous conversations where required.
5. Separate durable moderation records from private conversation content where possible.
6. Provide understandable privacy information to users.
7. Define age eligibility and appropriate safeguards.
8. Implement accessible reporting and blocking workflows.
9. Establish moderator permissions and administrative audit records.
10. Restrict access to identifying information.
11. Avoid capturing private messages in analytics or error reports.
12. Document third-party data sharing, including AI and media providers.
13. Implement applicable deletion and data-request procedures.
14. Secure backups containing data that must be retained.
15. Test privacy controls and deletion behavior.

